<h3>What this unit is about</h3>
<p>Before we begin, a word about where you’re starting from. If you arrived here thinking you already know what to make of Karl Marx — either that he was a prophet who exposed the fundamental injustice of capitalism, or that he was the intellectual architect of some of the twentieth century’s worst regimes — you are in good company. Marx provokes strong reactions, and those reactions tend to arrive before the reading does.</p>
<p>This unit will ask you to set that aside, temporarily, and approach Marx the way you have approached every philosopher in this course: as someone making an argument. What are his premises? Do they hold up? What does his conclusion actually say — not what later movements said in his name, but what the text in front of you says? Philosophy requires that kind of careful attention, and it is especially important here, where the subject matter is charged enough that it’s easy to react without reading.</p>
<p>With that said: Marx is one of the most consequential thinkers in modern history. His influence on economics, sociology, political theory, and philosophy is staggering. You do not have to agree with him to benefit enormously from understanding him. And understanding him — really understanding him — requires engaging with his actual arguments, not with the caricatures that tend to pass for him in public debate.</p>
<h4>The shape of the argument</h4>
<p>Marx begins from a question that is still worth asking: <em>why do societies look the way they do?</em> Why do some people command vast wealth and others work themselves to exhaustion for wages that barely cover their needs? Why do the ideas that circulate in a society — about justice, freedom, merit, the natural order of things — so often turn out to benefit those already at the top?</p>
<p>Marx’s answer is structural. It is not primarily about the greed of bad people or the failures of individuals. It is about the way economic systems organize human relationships, and the way those relationships shape everything else: politics, law, religion, culture, and even the way people think about themselves. The system, he argues, doesn’t just distribute wealth unequally — it produces the ideas that make that unequal distribution seem natural, inevitable, or just.</p>
<p>The reading for this unit is the <em>Communist Manifesto</em>, co-written with Friedrich Engels in 1848. It is a short, fierce, brilliantly written document — one of the most widely read texts in modern history. It is also a philosophical text: it makes claims about the nature of history, the structure of capitalism, and the conditions for human freedom that deserve to be examined carefully, not just accepted or rejected on the basis of what they seem to threaten or promise.</p>
<p>The lecture pages will help you work through the argument section by section. By the end of the unit, you should be able to explain what Marx actually claimed — which is the necessary first step before deciding what you think of it.</p>
<div>
<p>← Looking back &nbsp;|&nbsp; Looking ahead →</p>
<p>In Unit 4, Hegel argued that history is the unfolding of Reason — that Spirit moves through time, realizing freedom in ever more adequate forms. He gave philosophy its grandest possible ambition: to comprehend the whole of human history as a rational process. Marx was a student of Hegel’s legacy, and he took the historical framework seriously. But he thought Hegel had it precisely backwards.</p>
<p>In Unit 6, Sartre will face the aftermath. If Marx is right that our values and beliefs are shaped by material conditions we did not choose, then in what sense are we free? Sartre’s existentialism is in part a response to this question — an insistence on radical freedom even in conditions that seem to foreclose it.</p>
</div>
